 interpreter from the very first moment. Evolution didn't build a vault. It built an incredibly sophisticated interpretive engine. Salience over fidelity. Yes. But I want to push on this because it's easy to just say biology interprets things, but the paper brings up a concrete example from developmental biology that really makes this unavoidable. It compares standard embryogenesis, how an embryo normally develops, with the induced formation of these things called anthrobots. Oh, the anthrobots are fascinating. Can we really dig into this? Because this example completely shattered my understanding of how DNA works. It absolutely breaks the standard engineering metaphor of DNA. So let's look at the work coming out of Michael Levin's lab. Normally, if you take cells from a frog embryo specifically, the cells that are destined to become the skin of the frog, they know what to do. Right. They become skin. Exactly. They form a passive barrier on the outside of the tadpole to keep bacteria out. That is their quote-unquote programmed job. Right. Standard embryogenesis. But Levin's team did something incredible. They scraped some of those skin cells off the embryo, dissociated them, and put them in a novel environment. Like a Petri dish. Yeah, a Petri dish. Yeah. Completely isolated from the rest of the embryo. No muscle cells, no nerve cells, just skin cells alone together. Okay, so going by the engineering mindset, if DNA is a blueprint, these cells should just sit there, right? Maybe form a flat layer of skin in the dish, or just die because they are missing the rest of the blueprint's contact. Exactly. That's what you'd expect. But that is not what happens. Instead of forming a flat passive layer, these cells get together and reboot their multicellularity. Reboot? Yeah, they form entirely new independent living structures called xenobots or anthrobots if they're derived from human cells. They form a spherical body. Whoa. And they take the little hairs, called cilia, whi