ah which is used to", "tokens": [50973, 281, 829, 257, 8482, 3481, 411, 746, 1219, 257, 261, 1053, 260, 3481, 261, 1053, 260, 3481, 1338, 597, 307, 1143, 281, 51233], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.05421842649145034, "compression_ratio": 1.7231833910034602, "no_speech_prob": 2.0635750053177304e-12}, {"id": 260, "seek": 151592, "start": 1533.28, "end": 1539.2, "text": " model brownian motion you'd put it directly on the infinite space of all possible future trajectories", "tokens": [51233, 2316, 6292, 952, 5394, 291, 1116, 829, 309, 3838, 322, 264, 13785, 1901, 295, 439, 1944, 2027, 18257, 2083, 51529], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.05421842649145034, "compression_ratio": 1.7231833910034602, "no_speech_prob": 2.0635750053177304e-12}, {"id": 261, "seek": 151592, "start": 1539.2, "end": 1544.96, "text": " it feels natural because it handles continuous time but fliction provides a brief devastating proof", "tokens": [51529, 309, 3417, 3303, 570, 309, 18722, 10957, 565, 457, 932, 4105, 6417, 257, 5353, 21280, 8177, 51817], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.05421842649145034, "compression_ratio": 1.7231833910034602, "no_speech_prob": 2.0635750053177304e-12}, {"id": 262, "seek": 154592, "start": 1545.92, "end": 1551.2, "text": " why this is mathematically bankrupt for physical systems why because trajectories in our continuation", "tokens": [50365, 983, 341, 307, 44003, 21780, 337, 4001, 3652, 983, 570, 18257, 2083, 294, 527, 29357, 50629], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.058477835221724075, "compression_ratio": 1.6875, "no_speech_prob": 2.2052565936542257e-12}, {"id": 263, "seek": 154592, "start": 1551.2, "end": 1558.16, "text": " spaces by definition must be absolutely continuous they have a bounded variation because they represent", "tokens": [50629, 7673, 538, 7123, 1633, 312, 3122, 10957, 436, 362, 257, 37498, 12990, 570, 436, 2906, 50977], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.058477835221724075, "compression_ratio": 1.6875, "no_speech_prob": 2.2052565936542257e-12}, {"id": 264, "seek": 154592, "start": 1558.16, "end": 1563.6000000000001, "text": " real physical velocities let's ground that bounded variation just means things can't instantly", "tokens": [50977, 957, 4001, 7806, 1088, 718, 311, 2727, 300, 37498, 12990, 445, 1355, 721, 393, 380, 13518, 51249], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.058477835221724075, "compression_ratio": 1.6875, "no_speech_prob": 2.2052565936542257e-12}, {"id": 265, "seek": 154592, "start": 1563.6000000000001, "end": 1569.76, "text": " teleport back and forth infinitely fast yeah like a car can't be going 60 miles an hour forward and then", "tokens": [51249, 28050, 646, 293, 5220, 36227, 2370, 1338, 411, 257, 1032, 393, 380, 312, 516, 4060, 6193, 364, 1773, 2128, 293, 550, 51557], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.058477835221724075, "compression_ratio": 1.6875, "no_speech_prob": 2.2052565936542257e-12}, {"id": 266, "seek": 156976, "start": 1569.76, "end": 1576.32, "text": " instantaneously be going 60 miles an hour backward in the same microsecond exactly physical systems have", "tokens": [50365, 9836, 13131, 312, 516, 4060, 6193, 364, 1773, 23897, 294, 264, 912, 3123, 37841, 18882, 2293, 4001, 3652, 362, 50693], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.06635392748791238, "compression_ratio": 1.5669291338582678, "no_speech_prob": 2.0479585909061182e-12}, {"id": 267, "seek": 156976, "start": 1576.32, "end": 1583.84, "text": " inertia they have limits but a typical path under a wiener measure true brownian randomness like dust", "tokens": [50693, 37234, 436, 362, 10406, 457, 257, 7476, 3100, 833, 257, 261, 1053, 260, 3481, 2074, 6292, 952, 4974, 1287, 411, 8634, 51069], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.06635392748791238, "compression_ratio": 1.5669291338582678, "no_speech_prob": 2.0479585909061182e-12}, {"id": 268, "seek": 156976, "start": 1583.84, "end": 1590.64, "text": " motes jittering in a sunbeam or idealized stock market ticks is nowhere differentiable and has", "tokens": [51069, 2184, 279, 361, 3904, 278, 294, 257, 3295, 650, 335, 420, 7157, 1602, 4127, 2142, 42475, 307, 11159, 819, 9364, 293, 575, 51409], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.06635392748791238, "compression_ratio": 1.5669291338582678, "no_speech_prob": 2.0479585909061182e-12}, {"id": 269, "seek": 156976, "start": 1590.64, "end": 1596.16, "text": " unbounded variation meaning what visually if you zoomed in on it it would look infinitely jagged", "tokens": [51409, 517, 18767, 292, 12990, 3620, 437, 19622, 498, 291, 8863, 292, 294, 322, 309, 309, 576, 574, 36227, 6368, 3004, 51685], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.06635392748791238, "compression_ratio": 1.5669291338582678, "no_speech_prob": 2.0479585909061182e-12}, {"id": 270, "seek": 159616, "start": 1596.16, "end": 1601.3600000000001, "text": " because of this fundamental mismatch between real physics and pure randomness the wiener measure", "tokens": [50365, 570, 295, 341, 8088, 23220, 852, 1296, 957, 10649, 293, 6075, 4974, 1287, 264, 261, 1053, 260, 3481, 50625], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.0571285328775082, "compression_ratio": 1.8327272727272728, "no_speech_prob": 2.8655504618474437e-12}, {"id": 271, "seek": 159616, "start": 1601.3600000000001, "end": 1607.2, "text": " assigns exactly zero probability to the set of absolutely continuous paths wait so if you try to", "tokens": [50625, 6269, 82, 2293, 4018, 8482, 281, 264, 992, 295, 3122, 10957, 14518, 1699, 370, 498, 291, 853, 281, 50917], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.0571285328775082, "compression_ratio": 1.8327272727272728, "no_speech_prob": 2.8655504618474437e-12}, {"id": 272, "seek": 159616, "start": 1607.2, "end": 1613.52, "text": " measure your continuation space using that method the math just spits out zero every single time it", "tokens": [50917, 3481, 428, 29357, 1901, 1228, 300, 3170, 264, 5221, 445, 637, 1208, 484, 4018, 633, 2167, 565, 309, 51233], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.0571285328775082, "compres